{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 21, :comments ["<b>And Jacob said, [etc.]: Give me, [etc.]</b> The reason he said <b>and I will come to her</b> — and it is language unfitting for the righteous man of the world — may be explained along the lines of what Rambam wrote in chapter 5 of Laws of Marriage: ״You are betrothed to me by this work that I will do with you,״ and he did it — she is not betrothed, etc., for any wage is a loan, and one who betroths through a loan — she is not betrothed. Hence he said ״Give me״ etc., for the reason that I did not acquire her through what I labored, and I need to acquire her through intercourse, for a woman is acquired through intercourse. And that is his saying <b>and I will come to her</b> — for through the labor he did not acquire her, since it was a loan, and one who betroths through a loan — she is not betrothed." "Or it may say along this line: Do you not find that Rachel said, ״Are we not considered strangers to him, for he has sold us״ (Genesis 31:15), and Rambam wrote (Laws of Sale, chapter 7) that one may acquire slaves and land through a loan, even though the money is not present [as cash], up to here. And that is his saying <b>Give me my wife</b> — meaning: she is already my wife, for I hold according to Rambam, and the situation before us constitutes a valid sale, and I have already acquired her even though the money is not extant. And what I say to you ״Give me״ — is not for completion of acquisition, for she is already acquired, but rather to come to her in the way of all the earth. And that is his saying <b>and I will come to her</b> — to remove from his [Laban's] mind any thought that she is not yet acquired."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 29, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:44:51.805889958Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}